# Deliberately empty of prunes, and the two absences are worth naming so
# neither gets "fixed" back in.
#
# 1. The two externalized Thompson tables (freezeH2O.dat, qr_acr_qg_V4.dat)
#    were excluded here through 2.4.1 because without those lines this
#    sdist exceeded PyPI's 100 MiB per-file cap.  The directory holding
#    them moved to the `gpuwm-data` companion distribution in 2.5.0, so
#    the exclusions moved with it: see gpuwm-data/MANIFEST.in.  There is
#    nothing under gpuwm/ left to exclude.
#
# 2. The top-level `configs/` directory reaches the sdist already:
#    setuptools folds `[tool.setuptools.package-data]` into the source
#    distribution's default file set, so the one declaration that puts the
#    97 repository configs in the wheel puts them in the sdist too
#    (measured, not assumed -- tests/test_configs_are_packaged.py runs
#    sdist._add_defaults_python and counts them).
#    docs/public/VERIFICATION.md's reproduce recipe and docs/public/LES.md
#    both name files under it, so a prune here would make those pages
#    unfollowable from a source build.
#
# tests/test_package_data_coverage.py pins this file against the wheel
# exclusion lists in both pyprojects.

# The ONE rule left, and it is about the OTHER distribution rather than
# about any file's size or license.  `gpuwm-data/` belongs to the
# companion distribution built from `gpuwm-data/pyproject.toml`, and a
# fragment of it inside gpuwm's own artifacts is worse than either whole
# answer: a build carrying `gpuwm_data/__init__.py` and none of its data
# is a package that says where the tables are and has none.
#
# BOTH artifacts leaked it, by two different mechanisms, and this line
# only ever closed one of them:
#
#   sdist -- setuptools' default file set sweeps every .py under the
#     project root, which put `gpuwm-data/gpuwm_data/__init__.py`, and
#     nothing else, inside gpuwm's sdist.  `prune` is the fix and is
#     below.
#   wheel -- [tool.setuptools.packages.find]'s `include` was `gpuwm*`,
#     which is fnmatch over directory names and matched `gpuwm-data`.
#     An earlier version of this comment asserted the opposite -- that
#     `gpuwm-data` is not a valid identifier so no wheel could contain
#     it -- and the built wheel disproved it: a pyproject `find` defaults
#     to `namespaces = true`, and a namespace finder needs no identifier.
#     Fixed where it belongs, in pyproject.toml's `include`/`exclude`,
#     and gated by tests/test_package_data_coverage.py.
prune gpuwm-data

# The staged Rust artifacts are wheel payload, and a SOURCE
# distribution has no business carrying one platform's executables.
# Measured on the 2.5.0 Linux shakeout: `python -m build` after
# `tools/stage_wheel_bridges.py --platform linux-x86_64` swept all 18
# native ELF artifacts in here -- the sdist went from 95,236,252 B to
# 114,906,421 B and shipped Linux binaries as source.  `package-data`
# sweeps the directory into the wheel deliberately (that is the whole
# platform-wheel design) and setuptools folds the same declaration into
# the sdist's default file set, so this is the line that separates the
# two.  setup.py additionally REFUSES an sdist of a staged tree: with
# the prune alone, `python -m build` would build its wheel from the
# pruned sdist and quietly produce a py3-none-any wheel while the
# operator was staging for a platform one.
# tests/test_sdist_excludes_staged_bridges.py measures both halves.
prune gpuwm/libexec/bridges
